00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,700
This lesson develops an interview-ready approach to Hot Keys and Uneven Load. The central model is: Detect and

2
00:00:13,750 --> 00:00:27,450
diffuse disproportionate load concentrated on a small set of keys or tenants. Begin by clarifying the product behavior

3
00:00:27,500 --> 00:00:41,200
rather than naming technologies. Ask who uses the system, what the critical write and read paths are, which

4
00:00:41,250 --> 00:00:54,950
operations must be correct, and which outcomes may be delayed. State two or three non-goals. This prevents a

5
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:08,700
familiar architecture from being forced onto the wrong product. Separate functional requirements from quality attributes. Functional requirements describe

6
00:01:08,750 --> 00:01:22,450
user-visible actions and state changes. Quality attributes describe

7
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:36,200
scale, latency, availability, durability, consistency, privacy, and cost. Pick the two attributes that drive architecture and say why.

8
00:01:36,250 --> 00:01:49,950
Avoid promising maximum consistency, maximum availability, minimum latency, and minimum cost simultaneously. A strong interview answer makes the

9
00:01:50,000 --> 00:02:03,700
tradeoff visible and ties it to a user consequence. Use rounded assumptions for daily active users, peak requests

10
00:02:03,750 --> 00:02:17,450
per second, read-to-write ratio, payload size, retention, and replication. Calculate only values that influence a decision. Label every

11
00:02:17,500 --> 00:02:31,200
assumption and keep units on the page. An estimate is useful when it changes partition count, storage tier,

12
00:02:31,250 --> 00:02:44,950
cache capacity, or network design. It is not

13
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:58,700
useful when it creates false precision. Add headroom and distinguish average from peak load. Build the design around

14
00:02:58,750 --> 00:03:12,450
these decisions: Replication, request coalescing, adaptive partitioning, caching, and quotas. Draw clients, edge controls, stateless services, authoritative stores,

15
00:03:12,500 --> 00:03:26,200
asynchronous paths, and observability. Put arrows in the direction of data flow and label synchronous versus asynchronous work.

16
00:03:26,250 --> 00:03:39,950
For each durable write, identify the source of truth. For each cache or index, identify how it becomes

17
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:53,700
fresh enough. For each queue, identify the consumer's idempotency strategy. Define the smallest useful API and the core

18
00:03:53,750 --> 00:04:07,450
data entities. Include identifiers, ownership, timestamps or versions,

19
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:21,200
and uniqueness constraints. Explain the dominant query patterns before selecting storage. If data is partitioned, name the partition

20
00:04:21,250 --> 00:04:34,950
key and show why it spreads load without breaking essential queries. If an operation crosses partitions or services,

21
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:48,700
describe the consistency boundary and how partial completion is reconciled. Pressure-test the design against Single-shard saturation, synchronized misses,

22
00:04:48,750 --> 00:05:02,450
and moving the hotspot downstream. Walk through timeout, retry, dependency outage, overload, and recovery. Bound every retry with

23
00:05:02,500 --> 00:05:16,200
deadlines, backoff, and jitter. Use idempotency where a repeated request can create a duplicate effect. Define whether the

24
00:05:16,250 --> 00:05:29,950
system fails closed, fails open, serves stale data,

25
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:43,700
queues work, or rejects it. Then explain how operators detect the condition and how the system returns to

26
00:05:43,750 --> 00:05:57,450
normal. For every major component, connect the choice to a requirement and name the cost it introduces. A

27
00:05:57,500 --> 00:06:11,200
cache reduces latency but creates freshness and invalidation work. Replication improves resilience and read capacity but introduces lag,

28
00:06:11,250 --> 00:06:24,950
conflict, and failover decisions. Partitioning increases scale but complicates queries, transactions, and rebalancing. Asynchronous work absorbs bursts but

29
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:38,700
changes completion semantics and requires retries, deduplication, and backlog operations. This requirement-choice-cost pattern keeps the answer grounded and

30
00:06:38,750 --> 00:06:52,450
prevents architecture by buzzword. Start with the simplest

31
00:06:52,500 --> 00:07:06,200
architecture that satisfies the stated scale. Then identify the first bottleneck and evolve one dimension at a time:

32
00:07:06,250 --> 00:07:19,950
add caching for read pressure, partition state for size or write throughput, queue noncritical work for burst absorption,

33
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:33,700
and add regional redundancy only when the availability objective justifies the cost. This evolution story demonstrates judgment better

34
00:07:33,750 --> 00:07:47,450
than drawing every distributed component from the beginning. Define service-level indicators for latency, availability, correctness, freshness, and backlog.

35
00:07:47,500 --> 00:08:01,200
Alert on user impact and exhaustion trends, not every internal fluctuation. Carry request identifiers through logs and traces.

36
00:08:01,250 --> 00:08:14,950
For security, authenticate the caller, authorize the resource

37
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:28,700
action, encrypt in transit and at rest, minimize sensitive data, rotate credentials, and audit privileged changes. Mention abuse

38
00:08:28,750 --> 00:08:42,450
controls when the system exposes public APIs or user-generated content. Prepare to zoom into one write and one

39
00:08:42,500 --> 00:08:56,200
read at message-level detail. Name the request, validation, authoritative mutation, secondary updates, response, and recovery behavior. Then answer

40
00:08:56,250 --> 00:09:09,950
four predictable pivots: what changes at ten times scale, what happens when the main dependency is unavailable, how

41
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:23,700
duplicate requests are handled, and how a user observes a stale or partial result. If a decision depends

42
00:09:23,750 --> 00:09:37,450
on an unknown requirement, offer two branches and

43
00:09:37,500 --> 00:09:51,200
ask the interviewer which product behavior to optimize. Close by restating the requirements that drove the design, the

44
00:09:51,250 --> 00:10:04,950
source of truth, the main data path, and the most important tradeoff. Offer one alternative and say when

45
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:18,700
it would be preferable. Then invite the interviewer to choose a deep dive: data model, consistency, capacity, failure

46
00:10:18,750 --> 00:10:32,450
recovery, or a product-specific edge case. The design is complete when its behavior under pressure is as clear

47
00:10:32,500 --> 00:10:46,200
as its happy path. Keep the final summary short enough that the interviewer can challenge one decision while

48
00:10:46,250 --> 00:10:59,950
